[{"id":126268,"sender_id":612,"sender_full_name":"nLab edit announcer","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/c58b62e30f75d369349d5c08eee8f6b9?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/2-Latest-Changes/topic/standard.20Borel.20space/near/126268\">#6</a> I don’t think the alternative definition (characterization result) is correct as stated so I removed the characterization result and the references associated with it (to works by Preston and Gray) </p>\n<p>The characterization result says that a measurable space is a standard Borel space iff its sigma-algebra is generated by a (countable) algebra that has a “basis” (whose key property involves a compact class property). (See Def. 5.2 in https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/categories+of+measure+theory for a definition of compact classes.) The claimed sources are an arXiv paper by Chris Preston and a book by Gray.</p>\n<p>As stated, the characterization result is false: It is easy to see that the sigma-algebras of countably infinite standard Borel spaces do not have the compact class property.</p>\n<p>For uncountable standard Borel spaces it is very much unclear that the characterization is correct. It does not follow from the claimed sources.</p>\n<p>To the best of my knowledge, the relevant result in Preston’s notes seems to be Theorem 6.1. It says that a measurable space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(X,\\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0993em;\">F</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is standard Borel iff (1) <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0993em;\">F</span></span></span></span> contains the singletons; (2) <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0993em;\">F</span></span></span></span> is countably generated; and (3) there is a function <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span></span></span></span> from <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span> to the Cantor space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=\\{0,1\\}^\\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0952em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">}</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8452em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathbb mtight\">N</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (endowed with its usual Borel <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">σ</span></span></span></span>-algebra <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0304em;\">B</span></span></span></span> such that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>:</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{F}=\\{f^{-1}(B): B \\in \\mathcal{B}\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0993em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7224em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0304em;\">B</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(X) \\in \\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0304em;\">B</span></span></span></span>. (Note that (3) implies (2).)</p>\n<p>I have not checked Preston’s proof but the characterization seems plausible in light of Theorem 2.1 of Mackey. Neither Preston’s proof nor Theorem 2.1 of Mackey delivers the claimed characterization result however. For one thing, the characterization in terms of the compact class property does not imply that the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">σ</span></span></span></span>-algebra contains the singletons (common to Mackey’s and Preston’s characterization).</p>\n<p>The book by Gray does not seem to have results that could deliver the claimed characterization result. The book covers the relation between Polish spaces and the Baire space (via Polish schemes). Certain subsets of the schemes have the compact class property but I don’t see how these results could lead to the claimed characterization. </p>\n<p>In any case, the results in Gray and Preston are very different (as I hope is clear from the above) so I don’t see how the text can claim that the characterization result is covered by both Gray and Preston.</p>\n<p>Mackey, Borel structure in groups and their duals, Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 85 (1957), 134-165</p>\n<p>meerkat</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/revision/diff/standard+Borel+space/10\">diff</a>, <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/revision/standard+Borel+space/10\">v10</a>, <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/standard+Borel+space\">current</a></p>","timestamp":1792740394,"subject":"standard Borel space","stream_id":2,"display_recipient":"Latest Changes","reactions":[]},{"id":126269,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/2-Latest-Changes/topic/standard.20Borel.20space/near/126269\">#7</a> I didn’t write the original wrong claim, but I think it can be fixed rather than deleting it outright. I have made some edits to the entry.</p>","timestamp":1792771946,"subject":"standard Borel space","stream_id":2,"display_recipient":"Latest Changes","reactions":[]}]